digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56641-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "A2" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given that the authors designed innovative membrane-labeling probes and optimized the ExM protocol to achieve dense membrane labeling and that the dense-labeling challenge is plausibly due to inadequacy of existing probes/protocols requiring redesign/optimization, therefore a plausible solution is to redesign membrane-labeling probes and optimize the ExM protocol to achieve dense membrane labeling in fixed tissue."];
  "D1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that uniform/continuous high-SBR membrane labeling makes membranes serve as landmarks for segmentation/tracing and that such a membrane-labeling method would enable nanoscale registration and facilitate segmentation/tracing on a confocal microscope, therefore achieving uniform/continuous high-SBR membrane labeling is a key enabling step for these analyses."];
  "D3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given that ExM is increasingly used to visualize proteins, nucleic acids, and membranes/lipids and that ExM can visualize anatomical features via dense total-protein labeling using NHS-ester staining, therefore ExM is a broadly adopted and versatile imaging platform, motivating extension to robust dense membrane labeling in fixed tissues."];
  "H1" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given that dense membrane labeling in fixed tissues is challenging and that developing new probe chemistry plus protocol optimization is a reasonable way to close such a capability gap, therefore a plausible explanation is that existing membrane-labeling probes/protocols are inadequate and must be redesigned/optimized."];
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given that ExM swelling pulls apart anchored biomolecules/labels isotropically (~4–10×) and that ExM separates diffraction-limited biomolecules/labels enough to be optically resolved, therefore infer the general capability that ExM turns nanoscale molecular proximity into resolvable spatial separation on optical microscopes."];
  "K1" [label="(0,0,0) If a scientific imaging field has a widely used method but a key target remains hard to label densely in fixed tissue, then developing a new labeling chemistry/probe plus protocol optimization is a reasonable approach to close the capability gap."];
  "K2" [label="(0,0,0) If membrane labeling is uniform/continuous with high signal-to-background, then membranes can serve as landmarks for ultrastructure preservation and for computational segmentation/tracing in 3D tissue images."];
  "K3" [label="(0,0,0) If ExM can physically magnify and isotropically separate anchored labels, then dense membrane-associated labels would be expected to reveal membrane ultrastructure on a confocal microscope (possibly aided by higher expansion and/or fluctuation imaging)."];
  "N10" [label="(1,1,7) Currently, if labels are covalently anchored to a swellable polymer network, then swelling can isotropically separate labels that are closer than the optical diffraction limit."];
  "N11" [label="(1,1,8) Currently, if labels are isotropically separated beyond the diffraction limit, then they can be optically resolved."];
  "N12" [label="(2,1,13) Currently, if ExM is performed iteratively to reach very high expansion, then conventional microscopes can achieve ~tens-of-nanometers (e.g., ~25 nm) effective resolution."];
  "N13" [label="(17,1,4) Currently, an ExM extension (ultrastructure expansion microscopy) allows visualization of preserved ultrastructures by optical microscopy."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, when the hydrogel swells in ExM, anchored biomolecules/labels are pulled apart isotropically, typically giving ~4–10× linear physical magnification."];
  "N3" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, ExM separates biomolecules/labels that were within the diffraction limit so they become far enough apart to be optically resolved."];
  "N4" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, iterative ExM can expand a sample a second time, yielding >10× linear magnification overall."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, ExM is increasingly used to visualize proteins, nucleic acids, and membranes/lipids."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, ExM can visualize anatomical features via dense total-protein labeling using NHS-ester staining."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, although multiple ExM methods exist for membrane/lipid labeling, achieving dense labeling in fixed tissues remains challenging."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, the ideal membrane-labeling outcome is uniform and continuous labeling with high signal-to-background, preserving ultrastructure while enabling visualization of associated proteins in fixed tissues."];
  "N9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, if such a membrane-labeling method existed, it would enable nanoscale registration of proteins to membrane landmarks and facilitate segmentation/tracing of membranous structures (e.g., axons, dendrites) on a confocal microscope."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to develop and evaluate ultrastructural membrane expansion microscopy by combining rational membrane-anchoring probes with an optimized expansion microscopy protocol for dense, continuous membrane labeling in fixed mouse brain tissue, enabling nanoscale confocal visualization of membranous ultrastructure together with associated biomolecular signals and motivating higher-resolution iterative expansion tests.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) Physical expansion, broad ExM applicability, dense membrane-labeling needs, and reported probe/protocol strategies jointly motivate developing and evaluating an ExM-based method for nanoscale membrane ultrastructure imaging in fixed tissue."];
  "P1" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, the authors designed innovative membrane-labeling probes and optimized the ExM protocol to achieve dense labeling of membranes (including plasma membranes) with ExM."];
  "P2" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, a strategy is reported to achieve the desired feature set (uniform/continuous membrane labeling, high SBR, ultrastructure preservation, and visualization of associated proteins)."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) The authors' redesigned membrane-labeling probes and optimized ExM protocol are plausibly a strategy for achieving dense, uniform, continuous, high-SBR membrane labeling with ultrastructure preservation and associated-protein visualization in fixed tissue."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) If ExM physically magnifies and isotropically separates anchored labels, then achieving uniform, continuous, high-SBR membrane labeling that preserves ultrastructure and permits associated-protein visualization would be expected to enable confocal visualization of membrane ultrastructure in fixed tissues."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) When labels are covalently anchored to a swellable polymer network, swelling can move initially sub-diffraction-separated labels far enough apart that they become optically resolvable."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The existence of iterative ExM with more than tenfold expansion, together with an ExM extension that visualizes preserved ultrastructures optically, motivates investigating whether higher-expansion ExM can support ultrastructural optical imaging."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Iterative ExM is a plausible route for pushing conventional-microscope imaging toward tens-of-nanometers effective resolution when it is used to reach very high expansion."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) A reported strategy that achieves uniform, continuous, high-SBR membrane labeling with ultrastructure preservation and associated-protein visualization is plausibly an implementation of the ExM membrane-labeling capability implied by the desired outcome."];
  "N7" -> "H1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
